Last summer I started playing Nethack. About 1 1/2 weeks after that, I 
ascended my first character. I play legitimately, but obviously I use every 
spoiler out there. After that first ascension, I lost interest in the game 
as college was starting up again, and I didn't play it until recently.

Fastfoward to about a week ago. I haven't played the game in a year, but I 
got the urge to download it again and try. I decided that Samurai's were 
fun, so I started making them. I died a lot, but eventually I got the rust 
worn off and got a character down to the Samurai quest where I was made 
quick work of by Ashikaga Takauji's sword. :)

I rolled another Samurai and went straight to town. I just ascended him a 
few minutes ago.

I am just wondering what it is that people have such a hard time with in 
this game. I feel that the beginning is by far the hardest, and after you 
clear the mines and sokoban, it is pretty hard to die unless it's by a 
stupid mistake (like my mistake of trying to go toe-to-toe with Ashikaga). 
With enough planning, you would really have to have horrible luck to die 
once you've gotten your character established. With reflection, which you 
can gain early on, you negate a good part of the damage in the game. Once 
you get magic resistance, you are pretty much set. Sure, having some good 
luck helps, but as long as you aren't needlessly wreckless, the game is 
easy. Just make sure you always have a way out of any given situation; carry 
unihorns, have a means of teleporting, carry a wand of digging for a quick 
exit, have healing potions on hand and keep your god happy just incase you 
need a helping hand from him. ;) There are some lessons you learn the hard 
way, but you'll never make them again. I've had my fair share of lessons 
too. I don't even go near an eel anymore without either genociding them or 
having an oilskin cloak. Oh yeah, and DON'T PRAY IN GEHENNOM. ;-)

Nethack is an excellent game. There are many ways out of almost any 
situation. Just play the game more like chess than DOOM and you will find it 
much easier.

... anyway, a short ascension log below.

The turn count is pretty high, and that has a lot to do with me having to 
backtrack through the entire dungeon a few times. I neglected to buy the 
candles, so I had to go back for those, and a surprise run in with a 
mindflayer had me running through the dungeon remapping about 8-9 levels. I 
also left all of my artifacts behind as well as a trunk full of other 
equipment.

Other than that, the game was pretty uneventful. The minetown had a magic 
lamp which became my lightsource for the rest of the game (IMO that is their 
best use). The castle /oW granted me Frost Brand. I had horrible luck with 
my ?oID and out of roughly 20 blessed ones, 2 of them ID'd my entire 
inventory, which was even more of a pain because of the mindflayer run in. 
It's worth noting that I didn't meet a single bones pile, which is pretty 
unusual for me (I use Hearse).

I cleared Ludios to get gold/protection, polymorphed some armor there to get 
the Hawaiian shirt, and that's pretty much it. By the time I was ready for 
the amulet run, I had a surplus of genocide scrolls so I decided to get rid 
of a few problems before they could show up, such as L's and R's.

 I had a pretty close call with Yeenoghu and Rodney in Juiblex's swamp on 
ascent after grabbing the amulet. He had me badly surrounded, and then that 
SOB Rodney knocked my +7 katana into the water. There was no way I was going 
to die, so I managed to get Yeenoghu to teleport away and I ran away and 
healed up a bit, then just avoided the pack and kept going. The Mysterious 
Force was a huge pain in the ass, and I probably got hit by it at least 10 
times. I didn't see Rodney again until the Plane of Earth, and that was 
that.

I got cornered by Famine early on, and he kept stunning me over and over. He 
did eventually take me out, but luckily I had an Amulet of Life Saving on. 
After that, it took a few blasts of / of death to get him down, and I walked 
right over to the altar and became a demigod, again.

